    "body": "## Why\nBoth recipes keep their dense FP8 GEMMs (and Qwen3 its experts) on Transformer Engine, and neither trains on MI355X:\n- DSv2-Lite FP8: TE's hipBLASLt wgrad GEMM returns `HIPBLAS_STATUS_INTERNAL_ERROR` in the first backward, or the first forward loss is NaN.\n- Qwen3-30B-A3B FP8: delayed scaling + legacy grouped GEMM hits an HSA memory aperture violation in iteration 3.\n\n## What\n- `use_turbo_gemm: true` and `use_turbo_grouped_gemm: true` in both recipes.\n- Qwen3: `fp8_recipe: tensorwise` (Turbo GEMMs reject `delayed`), legacy grouped GEMM dropped.\n- DSv2-Lite: `turbo_gemm_backend: \"fp8:triton\"`. Turbo's default FP8 tensorwise backend is hipBLASLt, which fails the same way on this model's backward GEMMs; Triton was faster than CK here (10.44 vs 10.71 s/iter).\n\n## Results (MI355X, 1 node, 50 iters, mock data, Turbo 53099da3 / #539)\n| recipe | s/iter | tokens/s/GPU | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Qwen3-30B-A3B FP8 | 8.76 | 29.9k | BF16 recipe on the same node: 9.36 s/iter, 28.0k |\n| DSv2-Lite FP8 | 10.47 | 43.8k | loss 11.86 -> 0.51 by iter 50, no NaN or skipped iters |\n\nBoth recipes also ran 10 iterations exactly as committed; the resolved Megatron arguments match the 50-iteration runs.",
